-- erwartet: C001
-- 1000 -- **a slot field reached straight off the table handle, and the C named a
-- member that does not exist.**
--
-- A `table T count 4 { slot { wert : u32, } }` lowers to exactly one struct with
-- exactly one member:
--
--     typedef struct { uint32_t wert; } T_slot;
--     typedef struct { T_slot slots[4]; } T;
--
-- `q->wert` on a `q : ptr<normal, r> T` passed `gabbro pruefe` with ZERO errors
-- and came out as `return q->wert;`. `cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror` at both
-- -O0 and -O2:
--
--     error: 'T' has no member named 'wert'
--
-- **It is the pattern this folder has paid for twice already**: a form the
-- checker admits, an emitter that writes something plausible for it, and a
-- refusal that says nothing. `gabbro emit` returned 0. *A silently wrong
-- lowering is worse than a refusal, because a refusal stands in the
-- certificate.*
--
-- WHY THE REFUSAL AND NOT A LOWERING
-- ----------------------------------
-- There is no lowering being withheld. `q->wert` does not name a place: a table
-- has `count` slots and a field belongs to ONE of them, so the spelling that
-- means something is `q.slots[i].wert` -- and it says which slot. The refusal
-- prints that repair.
--
-- COARSE IN THE SAFE DIRECTION («W9»)
-- -----------------------------------
-- The emitter asks only whether the FIRST suffix is `slots`, the one member
-- there is. It does not ask whether the name behind it is a declared slot field
-- -- that is a question about the declaration, and it belongs to the checker.
-- **That the checker gives no answer to `q->wert` is a second finding and is
-- named as one**; this file measures the emitter half.
--
-- Found on 2026-09-15 by walking the constructors of
-- `grammatik/Grammatik/Syntax.lean` and compiling what came out.
module gift::tabellenfeldampfeil {

table T count 4 {
    slot { wert : u32, }
}

impl fn lies(q : ptr<normal, r> T, i : index into T) -> u32
    effects { reads q }
    costs   <= 2 ops
{
    return q->wert;
}

}
